💡 **SUGGESTION: Include the underlying decoder message in the degradation diagnostic**

The new diagnostic is a static string, `'stored credential is not base64-encoded UTF-8'`. The two failure shapes it covers are distinguishable and cost nothing to separate: `base64.decode` throws `FormatException: Invalid character` / `Invalid length, must be multiple of four` (corrupt pipe), while `utf8.decode` throws `Unexpected extension byte (at offset N)` (valid base64, non-UTF-8 payload). Collapsing both into one string loses the offset/alphabet detail a Windows-only post-mortem would want.

```dart
} on FormatException catch (error) {
  return SecureKeyReadOutcome(
    name,
    SecureKeyReadStatus.error,
    error: 'stored credential is not base64-encoded UTF-8: ${error.message}',
  );
}
```

`SecureKeyReadOutcome.error` already carries stderr tails and exit codes elsewhere and never contains the secret — a decoder message keeps that contract (it names offsets/characters of the encoded text, not the payload).

